---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import {CHROMATIC} from '../../lib/music.js';
---
<BaseLayout title="Piano Chord Finder by Notes | Free Interactive Tool" description="Choose 3 or 4 piano notes to identify possible major, minor, diminished, augmented and seventh chords. Works in your browser." canonicalPath="/tools/chord-finder/" crumbs={[{name:'Chord Finder',href:'/tools/chord-finder/'}]}>
<div class="container page-hero"><span class="eyebrow">Free interactive tool</span><h1>Chord finder<br/>by piano notes.</h1><p class="lead">Know the notes but not the chord name? Choose any three or four keys. We'll identify possible chords using exact pitch-class matches and show alternative enharmonic spellings where appropriate.</p></div>
<section class="container section-compact"><div class="two-col"><div class="panel"><h2>Select your notes</h2><p>Choose a set of 3 or 4 distinct pitch classes. Octaves don't change the basic chord identification.</p><div class="toggle-grid" id="pitch-picker">{CHROMATIC.map(p=><button class="pitch-toggle" type="button" aria-pressed="false" data-pitch={p.pc}>{p.label}</button>)}</div><div class="button-row no-print"><button class="btn" type="button" id="clear-pitches">Clear selection</button><button class="btn" type="button" id="example-pitches">Try C–E–G</button></div><p class="help-text">Chord names can be ambiguous without a bass note, key and harmonic context.</p></div><aside class="panel"><h2>Possible matches</h2><div id="finder-results" class="results-box" aria-live="polite">Select three or four notes to see matching chords.</div><p class="help-text" style="margin-top:12px">A chord may share its sound with an enharmonic spelling; our results explain that distinction instead of pretending there is always one name.</p></aside></div></section>
<section class="container section-compact prose"><h2>How the chord finder works</h2><p>The finder compares your selected pitch classes with common major, minor, augmented, diminished and seventh-chord formulas in twelve-tone equal temperament. It does not attempt to identify every possible jazz voicing or suspended chord. For slash chords and inversions, the lowest played note also matters, so use the <a class="text-link" href="/tools/inversion-explorer/">inversion explorer</a> if you know the bass.</p></section>
</BaseLayout>
